  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Multiflora rose rust (Phragmidium rosae-multiflorae) is a fungal pathogen in the Phragmidiaceae family that specifically infects multiflora rose plants, causing rust-colored pustules on leaves and stems. This rust fungus is particularly problematic in the moist climate of New England, where it can significantly weaken host plants.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Orange-red pustules on leaf undersides",
      "description": "Look for bright orange or rust-colored, powdery pustules on the underside of rose leaves, which is the telltale sign of active rust infection.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Yellow spots on upper leaf surface",
      "description": "Infected leaves often develop chlorotic (yellowish) spots on the upper surface corresponding to pustule locations underneath.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Affected stems and petioles",
      "description": "Dark, elongated lesions may appear on rose stems and leaf petioles as the disease progresses.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "This rust thrives on multiflora rose plants in the humid, temperate climate of New England, particularly in areas with high moisture and moderate temperatures that favor fungal spore development.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Phragmidium rosae-multiflorae has a complex life cycle involving multiple spore stages (aecia, uredinia, and telia), making it a macrocyclic rust that requires specific environmental conditions to complete its development on rose hosts.",
